#: ../../source/undici_README.md:525
msgid "Specification Compliance"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/undici_README.md:527
msgid ""
"This section documents parts of the [HTTP/1.1](https://www.rfc-"
"editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html) and [Fetch "
"Standard](https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org) that Undici does not support or "
"does not fully implement."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/undici_README.md:530
msgid "CORS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/undici_README.md:532
msgid ""
"Unlike browsers, Undici does not implement CORS (Cross-Origin Resource "
"Sharing) checks by default. This means:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/undici_README.md:534
msgid "No preflight requests are automatically sent for cross-origin requests"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/undici_README.md:535
msgid "No validation of `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` headers is performed"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/undici_README.md:536
msgid "Requests to any origin are allowed regardless of the source"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/undici_README.md:538
msgid ""
"This behavior is intentional for server-side environments where CORS "
"restrictions are typically unnecessary. If your application requires "
"CORS-like protections, you will need to implement these checks manually."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/undici_README.md:540
msgid "Garbage Collection"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/undici_README.md:542
msgid "https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#garbage-collection"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/undici_README.md:544
msgid ""
"The [Fetch Standard](https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org) allows users to skip "
"consuming the response body by relying on [garbage "
"collection](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-"
"US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Memory_Management#garbage_collection) to release "
"connection resources."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/undici_README.md:547
msgid ""
"Garbage collection in Node is less aggressive and deterministic (due to "
"the lack of clear idle periods that browsers have through the rendering "
"refresh rate) which means that leaving the release of connection "
"resources to the garbage collector can lead to excessive connection "
"usage, reduced performance (due to less connection re-use), and even "
"stalls or deadlocks when running out of connections. Therefore, __it is "
"important to always either consume or cancel the response body anyway__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/undici_README.md:565
msgid ""
"However, if you want to get only headers, it might be better to use "
"`HEAD` request method. Usage of this method will obviate the need for "
"consumption or cancelling of the response body. See [MDN - HTTP - HTTP "
"request methods - HEAD](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-"
"US/docs/Web/HTTP/Methods/HEAD) for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/undici_README.md:572
msgid "Note that consuming the response body is _mandatory_ for `request`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/undici_README.md:583
msgid "Forbidden and Safelisted Header Names"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/undici_README.md:585
msgid "https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#cors-safelisted-response-header-name"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/undici_README.md:586
msgid "https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#forbidden-header-name"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/undici_README.md:587
msgid "https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#forbidden-response-header-name"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/undici_README.md:588
msgid "https://github.com/wintercg/fetch/issues/6"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/undici_README.md:590
msgid ""
"The [Fetch Standard](https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org) requires "
"implementations to exclude certain headers from requests and responses. "
"In browser environments, some headers are forbidden so the user agent "
"remains in full control over them. In Undici, these constraints are "
"removed to give more control to the user."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/undici_README.md:592
msgid "Content-Encoding"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/undici_README.md:594
msgid "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#field.content-encoding"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/undici_README.md:596
msgid ""
"Undici limits the number of `Content-Encoding` layers in a response to "
"**5** to prevent resource exhaustion attacks. If a server responds with "
"more than 5 content-encodings (e.g., `Content-Encoding: gzip, gzip, gzip,"
" gzip, gzip, gzip`), the fetch will be rejected with an error. This limit"
" matches the approach taken by "
"[curl](https://curl.se/docs/CVE-2022-32206.html) and "
"[urllib3](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-gm62-xv2j-4rw9)."
msgstr ""

